  "text": "2016B08 Describe how the\nvacuum-insulated oxygen evaporator works.\nList:\n·\nDiagramme\n·\nFunction\n·\nPros\n·\nCons\nDiagramme\nHow it works\nStorage of O\n2\n:\n·\nCritical temp -118°C\n·\nBoiling point -180°C\n·\nProduced en masse by fractional distillation of\nair\n·\nTransported to VIE\n·\nStored as liquid with saturated\nvapour\n·\nKept outside in gated area\nIf pressure too low:\n(e.g. oxygen taken from pipeline supply)\n·\nIf mild drop: vapourisation of liquid\nO2\n·\nIf severe drop: liquid O2 passed through\npressure-raising vapouriser\n·\nVapourisation maintains low\ntemperature\n·\nCold gaseous O2 passed through superheater (just\ncoils exposed to ambient air, not actively heated)\n·\nAlarms\nIf pressure too high:\n·\nSafety release valve 7atm\n·\nPressure regulator 4atm\n·\nAlarms\nPros\n·\nLarge capacity compared with cylinder manifold\n·\nNo active cooling required, very energy\nefficient\nCons\n·\nExpensive\n·\nBig\nFeedback welcome at\nketaminenightmares@gmail.com",
